{"title":"datasette-llm 0.1a11","link":"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/14/datasette-llm/","date":1794700768000,"content":"\n    \n        <p><strong>Release:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/datasette/datasette-llm/releases/tag/0.1a11\">datasette-llm 0.1a11</a></p>\n        <p><code>datasette-llm</code> is the intermediate plugin for Datasette that supports configuring LLMs for use with other Datasette plugins.</p>\n<p>This release adds support for OpenAI-compatible endpoints, so you can configure Datasette to use an LLM hosted anywhere that talks the OpenAI pseudo-standard web protocol.</p>\n<p>This means that if you're running local models with <a href=\"https://lmstudio.ai/\">LM Studio</a> or <a href=\"https://ollama.com/\">Ollama</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp\">llama.cpp</a> (which just hit <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/blog/ggml-org/llama-cpp-v1\">v1.0.0</a>) you can run Datasette via <code>uvx</code> like this:</p>\n<div><pre>wget https://datasette.io/content.db\n\nuvx --with datasette-agent datasette \\\n  --root --secret 1 \\\n  -s plugins.datasette-llm.default_model <span><span>'</span>{</span>\n<span>    \"model\": \"lmstudio-qwen\",</span>\n<span>    \"model_name\": \"qwen/qwen3.8-27b\",</span>\n<span>    \"api_base\": \"http://localhost:1234/v1\"</span>\n<span>  }<span>'</span></span> content.db</pre></div>\n\n<p>And you'll get a local server running a Datasette Agent instance powered by your local model!</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of a chat interface. Header bar reads &quot;home&quot; on the left and &quot;root&quot; with a hamburger menu icon on the right. Below is a back arrow, the heading &quot;Chat&quot;, a model label &quot;lmstudio-qwen&quot; and an &quot;EXPORT&quot; button. A blue user message bubble says &quot;how many repos and how many releases are there?&quot; Below it is a collapsed tool call &quot;list_databases_and_tables&quot; and an expanded tool call &quot;sql_query&quot;. Its REQUEST section shows database &quot;content&quot;, display &quot;both&quot; and the SQL: SELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM repos) AS repo_count, (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM releases) AS release_count. Its RESPONSE section shows columns repo_count and release_count with a single row of 200 and 1889. Below that is a table with columns repo_count and release_count and one row: 200, 1889, followed by a &quot;View SQL query&quot; button. The assistant reply reads &quot;There are 200 repos and 1,889 releases in the content database.&quot; At the bottom is a text input with placeholder &quot;Type a message... (# to tag a table or query)&quot; and a blue &quot;Send&quot; button.\" src=\"/media/e797e7bc2196e062704ceb20b3216212d4fda0c11a031e612668cab3f286fbf3.webp\" /></p>\n    \n    \n        <p>Tags: <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/uv\">uv</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/datasette-agent\">datasette-agent</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/llm\">llm</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/datasette\">datasette</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/llms\">llms</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/ai\">ai</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/generative-ai\">generative-ai</a></p>\n    \n\n","author":"Simon Willison","siteTitle":"Simon Willison’s Weblog","siteHash":"1b599216bd27a00f15d67a3ed9719c64148630c70244b6dd8e743f17cb45527e","entryHash":"a0db9e1ecf706f3de2baa43ee8c5fb75f9d35b247ec1cc46dce84338c73d5b6d","category":"Tech"}